<header><p class="st-kicker">other · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Dominion of Newfoundland</h1><p class="st-meta">1907 CE – 1949 CE · capital St. John's</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's oldest colony, a self-governing dominion from 1907 that gave up self-government during the Depression and joined Canada in 1949.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-newfoundland">Colony of Newfoundland</a>, self-governing since 1855, had declined to join <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canada">Canada</a> in 1869. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-conference">Imperial Conference</a> of 1907 it was granted the status of a dominion, equal in title to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bond">Robert Bond</a>, who had sought a separate trade deal with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The economy depended on the salt cod fishery, with newer pulp, paper and iron mining. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-newfoundland-regiment">Newfoundland Regiment</a> was almost destroyed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaumont-hamel">Beaumont-Hamel</a> on 1 July 1916, and war debt, including a costly railway, weighed on the dominion. In 1927 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judicial-committee-of-the-privy-council">Privy Council</a> awarded it the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Depression</a> brought near-bankruptcy and riots against Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-squires">Richard Squires</a> in 1932.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1934 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> gave up self-government to a British-appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commission-of-government">Commission of Government</a>. American and Canadian bases brought prosperity in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. After two referendums in 1948, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joey-smallwood">Joey Smallwood's</a> campaign, it joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canada">Canada</a> as its tenth province on 31 March 1949.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dominion-of-newfoundland.jpg" alt="Dominion of Newfoundland" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Lrenhrda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Newfoundland_(1904%E2%80%931949).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1907 CE – 1949 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St. John's</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edward Morris, Richard Squires, Frederick Alderdice</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Roman Catholicism, Methodism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Newfoundland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=dominion-of-newfoundland" aria-label="Dominion of Newfoundland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3449 510 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dominion of Newfoundland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3769 766L3769 766M3769 766L4997 651M3769 766L3203 831"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3769 766h0M4997 651h0M3203 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3769 766h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3769 766h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks St. John's. <a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=dominion-of-newfoundland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
